  "$comment": "SHARED ACROSS TWO REPOSITORIES. Byte-identical copies live in optioniaWooCommercePlugin/tests/fixtures/shared/ and optioniaWooCommerceBackend/test/fixtures/shared/. Both are gated on this file's SHA-256 and on rule_case_count, so an edit to one fails both builds until the other matches. EVERY CASE DECLARES expect_passes. A fixture asserting only the final state would repeat 16b's lesson, where the pricing fixture proved both ends and neither language proved the middle: for rules the interesting failures are cascade depth and cap behaviour, and neither is visible in a final state. Every rule carries the FULL published shape, `sort_order` included \u2014 the fixture is what the PHP evaluator is built against, so a key the real document always carries and this one omits would be a shape only one language has seen. `option-set.serializer.spec.ts` asserts the serializer's key set against this file so the two cannot drift. One case reaches the cap and expects a REFUSAL with no partial state (ADR-050), because a truncated pass is a wrong price that looks right. See M17.2, ADR-050 and ADR-052.",
